# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Live environment state (NEVER COMMIT)
#
# Each provisioning generation writes a `.anti-demo*` directory holding the
# ownership manifest, Terraform state, plan archives, per-bout receipts, and
# server logs for one real cloud environment. These carry the AWS account ID,
# VPC/subnet/security-group/instance identifiers, RDS and Lakebase endpoint
# hostnames, secret ARNs, and the operator's identity. Terraform state is also
# the file most likely to gain a plaintext credential after a provider upgrade
# or a resource that does not support write-only attributes.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.anti-demo/
.anti-demo-v*/

# Terraform, anywhere in the tree. `infra/aws/.gitignore` repeats these for the
# module directory; they are duplicated here so a state file cannot be
# committed from a new location that has no local .gitignore of its own.
.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfplan
*.tfvars
!*.tfvars.example

# Secrets and runtime environment. `.env.example` is the committed template.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Server runtime artifacts. Logs from a session that authenticated to AWS and
# Databricks are treated as sensitive by default.
*.log
*.pid
*.launch.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Working artifacts (not source)
#
# Timestamped hand-made backups. Several agents used these as the only safety
# net before this repository had history; git replaces them. Keeping them out
# also stops a stale copy of a file from being mistaken for the live one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.backups/
*.bak
*.bak.*
*.orig
*.rej

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build output
#
# `frontend/dist` is deliberately excluded. It is generated by `npm run build`
# and is synced to a Databricks App from the working tree, not from a clone, so
# nothing in the deployment path needs it in git. Committing a fingerprinted
# bundle produces unreviewable diffs, constant merge conflicts, and — as this
# project has already hit — a stale bundle that silently disagrees with source.
#
# `frontend/dist-before` is a hand-made copy of that build output, kept while
# chasing a stale-bundle problem. It is excluded for the same reasons and one
# more: a snapshot of a bundle is stale the moment it is taken, so a reader who
# finds it cannot tell whether it or `src` is authoritative.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
frontend/dist/
frontend/dist-before/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Development-time screenshots
#
# `.shots/` holds PNGs captured while checking layout and contrast at various
# viewport sizes. They are evidence for a review that has already happened,
# they are large, and they go stale silently as the UI changes. The intentional
# artwork lives in `frontend/src/` and is committed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.shots/

# Dependencies
.venv/
venv/
node_modules/

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Files this tree must not contain
#
# Neither of these is a build artifact; both are ignored so that a tool which
# regenerates one cannot get it committed, and `bootstrap.sh` refuses to run
# while either exists. tests/test_deploy_hygiene.py asserts the same thing, so
# the ban survives someone deleting these lines.
#
# `requirements.txt`: its presence alone makes the Databricks Apps runtime
# install with pip on Python 3.11, whatever pyproject.toml asks for. This source
# uses a PEP 695 `type` alias, so it does not parse there — the container dies on
# a SyntaxError while the platform reports the deployment SUCCEEDED. Dependencies
# live in pyproject.toml and uv.lock.
#
# `.python-version`: uv discards a project environment built on any other
# interpreter. A stray one pinning 3.12 deleted this tree's provisioned 3.14
# environment and started rebuilding it seconds before a demo, which looks
# exactly like a hang. requires-python governs resolution instead, locally and
# in the deployed tree alike.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/requirements.txt
/.python-version

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Publication hardening
#
# Added while preparing this repository for publication. Every pattern here
# closed a gap proved with `git check-ignore --no-index`, not assumed by reading.
# The threat model is a stranger who clones the public repository, follows
# docs/BOOTSTRAP.md against their own AWS account, and then runs `git add -A` —
# at which point anything not matched below is staged, and a value removed in a
# later commit is still published forever.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Any generation directory, not just the `-v<n>` series. `.anti-demo/` and
# `.anti-demo-v*/` above cover today's two naming schemes; a directory named for
# anything else (`.anti-demo-scratch/`, `.anti-demo-2/`) matched neither and was
# committable. The generation directory is the single highest-value target in
# the tree: it holds the manifest, Terraform state, receipts and server logs for
# one real cloud environment.
.anti-demo*/

# `bootstrap.sh` writes `<manifest dir>/bootstrap.json`. That is normally inside
# a generation directory and so already covered, but the manifest directory is
# operator-chosen via ANTI_DEMO_MANIFEST — point it at any other path inside a
# clone and the state file was committable. It records the account and region
# the bootstrap ran against, so it is named here by filename as well as by
# location. Nothing tracked in this repository is called `bootstrap.json`.
bootstrap.json

# Long-lived AWS key material, under every name people actually use for it --
# including the ones somebody types by hand while setting this up and then
# forgets about. A file whose name announces that it holds credentials must never
# be stageable, so these are matched by name rather than waiting to be noticed.
aws_credentials
aws_credentials.*
aws_credentials_*
credentials.csv
*_accessKeys.csv

# The two banned files again, unanchored. The rooted `/requirements.txt` and
# `/.python-version` above state the deploy failure each one causes; those
# comments are correct and stay. What they do not cover is a nested copy —
# `frontend/.python-version` was not ignored — and uv reads the nearest one
# walking up from the working directory, so a nested file reproduces the same
# environment-destroying behaviour from a directory nobody thinks to check.
# `runner/requirements-round5.txt` is tracked and intentionally unaffected: it
# is a different filename and matches neither pattern.
**/requirements.txt
**/.python-version

# A private working note that must never become a commit. It is absent from a
# published clone by design, so if you are reading this there and wondering what
# it was: a runbook, written while preparing this repository, whose subject was
# which values must not be published. Documents about live identifiers attract
# live identifiers — this one once quoted two as evidence they were contained,
# which is the moment they stopped being contained — and it was untracked rather
# than ignored, so one `git add -A` would have made them permanent. Ignoring it
# closes that by mechanism instead of by vigilance. The trade is that `git clean
# -xfd` now destroys it unrecoverably, so
# tests/test_publish_runbook_is_ignored_and_present.py fails loudly if the file
# disappears or if this rule does. That test skips where the file is absent by
# design, which is why it is quiet for you.
/PUBLISH.md

# The agent handoff notes, for the same reason and by the same mechanism. Each is
# a dense session record of work against one real environment, so they carry
# instance, NAT, security-group, VPC and volume IDs, install-run IDs and public
# IPv4 addresses as a matter of course -- `HANDOFF-v8-teardown-inventory.md`
# alone held twenty-one of them. Scrubbing prose that dense will miss something,
# so these are withheld from publication rather than redacted, which is the same
# decision `tests/test_public_markdown_links.py` already encodes as
# `UNPUBLISHED_PREFIXES`. Matched unanchored so a note written in a subdirectory
# is covered too.
#
# This rule governs the *next* note. It does not untrack the nine that are
# already in the index -- .gitignore has no effect on a tracked path -- so those
# nine still ship unless the publish step withholds them, and they are still
# scanned by tests/test_no_live_identifiers_committed.py, which is what the
# `git rm --cached` note in tests/test_publish_runbook_is_ignored_and_present.py
# describes for `PUBLISH.md`.
HANDOFF-*.md

# The owner's internal findings log, withheld by the same settled decision and
# for the same reason: it is the densest record in the tree of what went wrong
# against real environments, so it carries live identifiers throughout. It had
# no rule at all until now -- Step 2 of `PUBLISH.md` excluded it by hand, which
# is vigilance rather than mechanism, and the publication repository is built by
# a fresh first commit where every path starts untracked and only .gitignore is
# consulted. Like the note rule above, this does not untrack the copy already in
# the index, and it must not: `tests/test_publish_runbook_is_ignored_and_present.py`
# uses that tracked copy as its marker for "this is the private working repo",
# and `git clean -xfd` leaves tracked files alone, which is what makes the marker
# survive a clean that destroys the ignored runbook beside it.
/OPEN-FINDINGS.md

# The remaining internal working documents, withheld by the same settled
# decision and closed the same way. `tests/test_public_markdown_links.py` already
# names the first two in `UNPUBLISHED_MARKDOWN`, so a link to either was known to
# 404 in the published copy while the file itself was still perfectly
# committable -- the exclusion existed as a documented intention and not as a
# mechanism. The last two had neither: both were untracked *and* unignored, which
# is the precise state the runbook rule above exists to prevent, because the
# publication repository is built by a fresh first commit where every path starts
# untracked and only .gitignore is consulted.
#
# None of these four is as dense with live identifiers as a handoff note --
# scanning them turns up none of the instance, VPC, security-group or ARN shapes
# `tests/test_no_live_identifiers_committed.py` looks for. They are withheld
# because of what they are: internal working state about one real installation.
# `DEMO-MORNING.md` is a run-of-show procedure carrying observed timings and the
# credential handling of a specific live install, and
# `docs/DEPLOYED-NETWORK-PATH.md` is an unapplied investigation into sealing that
# installation's network path.
#
# As with the two rules above, this does not untrack the copies of `TASKS.md` and
# `ROUND5_FIX_CHECKPOINT.md` already in the index, and it must not: .gitignore has
# no effect on a tracked path, and both are deliberately tracked here.
/TASKS.md
/ROUND5_FIX_CHECKPOINT.md
/DEMO-MORNING.md
/docs/DEPLOYED-NETWORK-PATH.md

# Tooling and OS
.databricks/
.DS_Store
*.swp
